{:edb/db-before-t 2683 :edb/db-after-t 2684 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535996 50 #inst "2026-10-09T12:33:14.932Z" 13194139535996 true] [17592186197971 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/assets/263853df5f080da778a34caacea945119099326f0ba27806d851f793416e9f92.png)\n\n \n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F} = \\langle 0, \\, x, \\, -z^2 \\rangle$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of the solid onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} \n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\,\\bbox[3pt, border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}} \\: \\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nDetermine the missing expression." 13194139535996 true] [17592186197971 1100 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F} = \\langle 0, \\, x, \\, -z^2 \\rangle$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of the solid onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as\n$\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\iint\\limits_D \\,\\bbox[3pt, border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}} \\: \\textrm{d}A$.\nDetermine the missing expression.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4144/Source/Images/020906e1438cc7bd21000dc9fa4ca331.png)" 13194139535996 false] [17592186197971 1145 "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface $S_1: z = g(x,y) = 0$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface $S_2: z = h(x,y) = -\\sqrt{4 - x^2 - y^2}$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper plane $S_1.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is upward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n} = \\langle 0,0,1 \\rangle.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨0,x,-{z}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}0\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower hemisphere $S_2.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z = h(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $\\mathbf{F}=\\langle P, \\, Q, \\, R \\rangle,$ then\n\n$$\nP = 0, \\qquad\nQ = x, \\qquad\nR = -z^2 = x^2 + y^2 - 4.\n$$\n\n \nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-x\\cdot \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+4\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+4\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535996 true] [17592186197971 1145 "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface $S_1: z = g(x,y) = 0$ is oriented upward, and\n- the lower surface $S_2: z = h(x,y) = -\\sqrt{4 - x^2 - y^2}$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper plane $S_1$. Since the positive orientation is upward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n} = \\langle 0,0,1 \\rangle$. As a result, we have\n$∬_(S_{1})F \\cdot dS|= ∬_(S_{1})F \\cdot ndS; = ∬_(D)⟨0, x,-z^{2}⟩ \\cdot ⟨0, 0, 1⟩dA; = ∬_(D) - z^{2}dA; = ∬_(D)0dA; = 0$.\n- Consider the lower hemisphere $S_2$.We compute the partial derivatives of $z = h(x,y){:}$\n$\\frac{∂h}{∂x}|= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(-\\sqrt{4 - x^{2} - y^{2}}) = \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4 - x^{2} - y^{2}}}; \\frac{∂h}{∂y}|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\sqrt{4 - x^{2} - y^{2}}) = \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4 - x^{2} - y^{2}}}$\nIf we denote $\\mathbf{F}=\\langle P, \\, Q, \\, R \\rangle$, then\n$P = 0, \\qquad Q = x, \\qquad R = -z^2 = x^2 + y^2 - 4$.\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n$∬_(S_{2})F \\cdot dS|= - ∬_(D)(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x} - Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y} + R)dA; = - ∬_(D)(-0 \\cdot \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4 - x^{2} - y^{2}}} - x \\cdot \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4 - x^{2} - y^{2}}} + x^{2} + y^{2} - 4)dA; = ∬_(D)\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{4 - x^{2} - y^{2}}} - x^{2} - y^{2} + 4dA$.\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)F \\cdot dS &= ∬_(S_{1})F \\cdot dS + ∬_(S_{2})F \\cdot dS \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)x y 4 - x 2 - y 2 - x 2 - y 2 + 4dA\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535996 false] [17592186197972 1100 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F} = \\langle x, \\, y, \\, 0 \\rangle$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the parabolic cylinder $z=1-x^2$ and the paraboloid $z=x^2+y^2$. Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as\n$\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\iint\\limits_D \\,\\bbox[3pt, border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}} \\: \\textrm{d}A$.\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" 13194139535996 false] [17592186197972 1100 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F} = \\langle x, \\, y, \\, 0 \\rangle$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the parabolic cylinder $z=1-x^2$ and the paraboloid $z=x^2+y^2.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} \n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\,\\bbox[3pt, border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}} \\: \\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" 13194139535996 true] [17592186197972 1145 "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface $S_1: z=g(x,y)=1-x^2$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface $S_2: z=h(x,y)=x^2+y^2$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper parabolic cylinder $S_1.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-{x}^{2})=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-{x}^{2})=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $\\mathbf{F}=\\langle P, \\, Q, \\, R \\rangle,$ then\n\n$$\nP = x, \\qquad\nQ = y, \\qquad\nR = 0.\n$$\n\n \nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-x\\cdot (-2x)-y\\cdot (0)+0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower paraboloid $S_2.$ \n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $\\mathbf{F}=\\langle P, \\, Q, \\, R \\rangle,$ then\n\n$$\nP = x, \\qquad\nQ = y , \\qquad\nR = 0.\n$$\n\n \nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-x\\cdot (2x)-y\\cdot (2y)+0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}-2{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535996 true] [17592186197972 1145 "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface $S_1: z=g(x,y)=1-x^2$ is oriented upward, and\n- the lower surface $S_2: z=h(x,y)=x^2+y^2$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper parabolic cylinder $S_1$.We compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y){:}$\n$\\frac{∂g}{∂x}|= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(1 - x^{2}) =-2x; \\frac{∂g}{∂y}|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(1 - x^{2}) = 0$\n\n> If we denote $\\mathbf{F}=\\langle P, \\, Q, \\, R \\rangle$, then\n> $P = x, \\qquad Q = y, \\qquad R = 0$.\n> As a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n> $∬_(S_{1})F \\cdot dS|= ∬_(D)(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x} - Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y} + R)dA; = ∬_(D)(-x \\cdot (-2x) - y \\cdot (0) + 0)dA; = ∬_(D)2x^{2}dA$.\n\n- Consider the lower paraboloid $S_2$. We compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y){:}$\n$\\frac{∂h}{∂x}|= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2} + y^{2}) = 2x; \\frac{∂h}{∂y}|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{2} + y^{2}) = 2y$\nIf we denote $\\mathbf{F}=\\langle P, \\, Q, \\, R \\rangle$, then\n$P = x, \\qquad Q = y, \\qquad R = 0$.\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n$∬_(S_{2})F \\cdot dS|= - ∬_(D)(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x} - Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y} + R)dA; = - ∬_(D)(-x \\cdot (2x) - y \\cdot (2y) + 0)dA; = - ∬_(D) - 2x^{2} - 2y^{2}dA; = ∬_(D)2x^{2} + 2y^{2}dA$.\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)F \\cdot dS &= ∬_(S_{1})F \\cdot dS + ∬_(S_{2})F \\cdot dS \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)2x^{2}dA + ∬_(D)2x^{2} + 2y^{2}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)4 x 2 + 2 y 2dA\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535996 false] [17592186198328 1020 #edb/ref 17592186378697 13194139535996 true] [17592186198328 1020 #edb/ref 17592186378704 13194139535996 true] [17592186198328 1020 #edb/ref 17592186378725 13194139535996 true] [17592186198328 1020 #edb/ref 17592186378732 13194139535996 true] [17592186198328 1020 #edb/ref 17592186378739 13194139535996 true] [17592186198329 1020 #edb/ref 17592186378711 13194139535996 true] [17592186198329 1020 #edb/ref 17592186378718 13194139535996 true] [17592186198329 1020 #edb/ref 17592186378746 13194139535996 true] [17592186198329 1020 #edb/ref 17592186378753 13194139535996 true] [17592186198548 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535996 true] [17592186198548 1100 "Consider the vector field $F = ⟨1, 0,-x⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the paraboloid $z = x^{2} + y^{2}$ and the plane $z = 1 + x$. Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as\n\n$∬_(S)F \\cdot dS = ∬_(D)A A A A A A AdA$.\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" 13194139535996 false] [17592186198548 1100 "Consider the vector field $F=⟨1,\\,0,\\,-x⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the paraboloid $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ and the plane $z=1+x.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" 13194139535996 true] [17592186198548 1145 "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=1+x$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper plane ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1+x)=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1+x)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=1,\\,Q=0,\\,R=-x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 0+(-x))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1-x)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider lower paraboloid ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=1,\\,Q=0,\\,R=-x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-1\\cdot 2x-0\\cdot 2y+(-x))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-2x-x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1-x)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}3x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2x-1\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535996 true] [17592186378697 1017 #edb/ref 17592186378698 13194139535996 true] [17592186378697 1089 "q-161895" 13194139535996 true] [17592186378697 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535996 true] [17592186378697 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/assets/0e374f18d8b87de2fddd9a2127ee84709746c7c1b75083441afcc7b6c79402fb.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨-z,\\,0,\\,x⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" 13194139535996 true] [17592186378697 1113 #uuid "87242b70-612b-5db0-9c6f-2022ddd8e964" 13194139535996 true] [17592186378697 1145 "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So, \n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=0$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper paraboloid ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=-z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5,\\,Q=0,\\,R=x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5)\\cdot (-2x)-0\\cdot (-2y)+x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-10x+x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-9x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower plane ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is downward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,-1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨-z,0,x⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-9x)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}-x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-10x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535996 true] [17592186378698 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378699 13194139535996 true] [17592186378698 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378700 13194139535996 true] [17592186378698 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378701 13194139535996 true] [17592186378698 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378702 13194139535996 true] [17592186378698 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378703 13194139535996 true] [17592186378698 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535996 true] [17592186378698 1041 "selection" 13194139535996 true] [17592186378698 1104 #edb/ref 17592186378703 13194139535996 true] [17592186378698 1110 #uuid "ddf521ea-fc63-5207-ab84-eb3a3c45490f" 13194139535996 true] [17592186378699 1034 "4{x}^{3}-4x{y}^{2}+10x" 13194139535996 true] [17592186378699 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535996 true] [17592186378699 1111 #uuid "d30fd415-bef5-5bad-854f-586531d53c94" 13194139535996 true] [17592186378700 1034 "2{x}^{3}+4x{y}^{2}-6x" 13194139535996 true] [17592186378700 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535996 true] [17592186378700 1111 #uuid "8cba9584-7c2c-51c1-8955-b32bb09d86b0" 13194139535996 true] [17592186378701 1034 "-2{x}^{3}-6x{y}^{2}-6x" 13194139535996 true] [17592186378701 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535996 true] [17592186378701 1111 #uuid "e426fb22-8e31-596a-a5a4-0b9ed2a693f6" 13194139535996 true] [17592186378702 1034 "-4{x}^{3}-2x{y}^{2}+6x" 13194139535996 true] [17592186378702 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535996 true] [17592186378702 1111 #uuid "04b66e8b-f9a7-5730-b513-8a9797591ab6" 13194139535996 true] [17592186378703 1034 "2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-10x" 13194139535996 true] [17592186378703 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535996 true] [17592186378703 1111 #uuid "eb1abf14-3421-564c-8532-63312b865da5" 13194139535996 true] [17592186378704 1017 #edb/ref 17592186378705 13194139535996 true] [17592186378704 1089 "q-161901" 13194139535996 true] [17592186378704 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535996 true] [17592186378704 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/assets/c7f140ad2c5687db48171819e1efaa858701af881ebbee57ff835da569f30947.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨{z}^{2},\\,0,\\,y⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" 13194139535996 true] [17592186378704 1113 #uuid "e3eea45a-e598-5192-b757-2e1b9a1153d5" 13194139535996 true] [17592186378704 1145 "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So, \n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=0$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper hemisphere ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP={z}^{2}=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2},\\,Q=0,\\,R=y.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}})-0\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}})+y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}+y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower plane ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is downward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,-1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨{z}^{2},0,y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}+y\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}-y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535996 true] [17592186378705 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378706 13194139535996 true] [17592186378705 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378707 13194139535996 true] [17592186378705 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378708 13194139535996 true] [17592186378705 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378709 13194139535996 true] [17592186378705 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378710 13194139535996 true] [17592186378705 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535996 true] [17592186378705 1041 "selection" 13194139535996 true] [17592186378705 1104 #edb/ref 17592186378706 13194139535996 true] [17592186378705 1110 #uuid "9423072b-912c-5bf1-beea-06b077cc7f29" 13194139535996 true] [17592186378706 1034 "x\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}" 13194139535996 true] [17592186378706 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535996 true] [17592186378706 1111 #uuid "1c498de6-9a5f-5702-9de8-add8ffe1b816" 13194139535996 true] [17592186378707 1034 "y\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}" 13194139535996 true] [17592186378707 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535996 true] [17592186378707 1111 #uuid "c3d5db3a-9b49-57d1-9674-7f7f712bd5b6" 13194139535996 true] [17592186378708 1034 "\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" 13194139535996 true] [17592186378708 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535996 true] [17592186378708 1111 #uuid "d7a30733-8953-5156-8058-2567c5d9c088" 13194139535996 true] [17592186378709 1034 "xy\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}" 13194139535996 true] [17592186378709 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535996 true] [17592186378709 1111 #uuid "e4135d67-f437-5326-82c8-5bfabe55338c" 13194139535996 true] [17592186378710 1034 "\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" 13194139535996 true] [17592186378710 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535996 true] [17592186378710 1111 #uuid "a54e41be-21f7-50c0-add8-402f24c9cfc9" 13194139535996 true] [17592186378711 1017 #edb/ref 17592186378712 13194139535996 true] [17592186378711 1089 "q-161904" 13194139535996 true] [17592186378711 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535996 true] [17592186378711 1100 "Consider the vector field $F=⟨-y,\\,0,\\,x⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the hemisphere $z=1-\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ and the plane $z=1.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" 13194139535996 true] [17592186378711 1113 #uuid "150a753b-c416-5943-8cfa-2d89a5b17df4" 13194139535996 true] [17592186378711 1145 "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=1$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=1-\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper plane ${S}_{1}.$\n\nSince the positive orientation is upward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨-y,0,x⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider lower hemisphere ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=-y,\\,Q=0,\\,R=x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-(-y)\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-(0)\\cdot \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}+x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}+x)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A-{∬}_{D}(\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}+x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x-\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535996 true] [17592186378712 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378713 13194139535996 true] [17592186378712 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378714 13194139535996 true] [17592186378712 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378715 13194139535996 true] [17592186378712 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378716 13194139535996 true] [17592186378712 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378717 13194139535996 true] [17592186378712 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535996 true] [17592186378712 1041 "selection" 13194139535996 true] [17592186378712 1104 #edb/ref 17592186378713 13194139535996 true] [17592186378712 1110 #uuid "025aa2b3-25c3-5a10-ae18-f680a03c33fd" 13194139535996 true] [17592186378713 1034 "-\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" 13194139535996 true] [17592186378713 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535996 true] [17592186378713 1111 #uuid "192c4460-e346